AB  - As large language models (LLMs) enhance the capabilities of multi-agent systems, they also introduce security risks that existing evaluation methods struggle to address in dynamic and complex interaction scenarios. This paper presents CogTrust, a cognitive logic-based framework for dynamic trust evaluation in LLM-based multi-agent systems. CogTrust analyzes three key dimensions of an agent’s consciousness, generation of content and actions based on cognitive logic. The framework incorporates a multi-weight subjective logic model with a time decay memory factor to balance short-term attack detection with long-term trust trends. The implementation accurately represents the trustworthiness of an agent in terms of dynamic reputation value quantification. To enhance system robustness, we embed reputation value into a resilient random perturbation defense mechanism that adapts to additive noise based on trust levels, effectively mitigating attacks while preserving system performance. Experiments conducted on popular LLM-based collaborative multi-agent systems with various LLMs demonstrate that CogTrust accurately indicates agents’ trust states, reducing the percentage of dangerous examples to 14% while maintaining a task success rate over 93%. We hope this research offers valuable insights into the research of trust systems within multi-agent environments.
