AB  - Tool-using large language model (LLM) agents turn inputs into proposed external actions, making query screening and call authorisation distinct security decisions. We evaluate this distinction in a seven-tool mock sandbox with TinyLlama-1.1B, Phi-3.5-mini, and Mistral-7B across 3,735 generations covering direct attacks, legitimate tool tasks, and indirect injection under a policy-by-tool-exposure factorial. Routing-corrected rule-positive calls occurred in 63/315, 147/315, and 109/315 direct-attack generations, whereas legitimate-task completion was 96/210, 188/210, and 204/210, respectively. In a four-arm call-level comparison, a deterministic label-blind gate blocked 363/365 rule-positive calls and retained 398/630 legitimate task completions, against 488/630 with no defence and 20/630 with query screening alone. Blinded annotation of a targeted 157-row sample showed strong inter-rater agreement on tool-call presence (κ=0.8637) and unsafe-call judgement (κ=0.9215). The unified parser achieved recall 0.9259 against human judgements, versus 0.5556 for the legacy parser. The indirect factorial yielded 5 parsed calls in 2,160 generations, 4 of them from benign documents; it therefore supports exact event-rate bounds rather than a policy-effect estimate. Trained on source-confounded data, the screen flagged 67/70 harmless tool-requesting tasks but 0/1,207 benign general prompts, behaviour consistent with sensitivity to tool vocabulary. Within the evaluated setting, these results support a layered design in which learned screening provides optional triage and deterministic call-level mediation governs execution.
