AB  - The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence (AI), especially large language models (LLMs) and LLM-based agents, has enabled a wide range of practical applications, while also introducing increasingly complex security risks. In particular, backdoor vulnerabilities in LLMs may propagate into agent-based systems, raising concerns about how such vulnerabilities behave within broader cyberattack settings across different system components. However, existing studies mainly focus on backdoor insertion during training or fine-tuning, with limited understanding of their interactions with downstream agent behaviors and their effects across diverse cyberattack types, model architectures, and key impact factors. To address this gap, this paper proposes a LLM backdoor-integrated cyberattack framework. We design a five-stage attack flow, characterizing the progression from poisoned data construction to final attack execution. We conduct in-depth analysis of the evolution of the attack surface, highlighting distinctions from previous attacks. Extensive empirical evaluations cover 5 representative LLM-based agent configurations and 4 realistic scenarios including simulation verification, flight control, database interaction, and remote healthcare. In each scenario, we evaluate feasibility and effectiveness, achieving success rates approaching or reaching 100%, while preserving agent functionality in benign conditions. Case studies under 3 cyberattacks cover the 4 aforementioned scenarios. Systematic analysis of impact factors reveals the influence of poisoning ratio, model scale, trigger design, fine-tuning method, data volume, and epoch on success and stealth of the proposed attack. These findings establish a strong empirical foundation for future defense strategies and offer critical insights into the evolving threat landscape of AI systems.
