AB  - Large language models (LLMs) may make building energy analytics more accessible, but their use in operational environments raises governance risks because high-resolution metering data may reveal occupancy patterns, routines, or other sensitive information. This paper presents a role-aware, locally deployed LLM architecture for policy-enforced building energy analytics. The architecture combines a web-based conversational interface, local LLM inference, Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools, and an external policy engine. The contribution is the architectural integration and prototype-level validation of local LLM inference, MCP-mediated tool access, external policy enforcement, role-aware context mediation, and audit logging for building-energy analytics; no novelty is claimed for these individual technologies. The architecture is evaluated in a real office-building case study using 5-second electricity metering data. A property-based validation protocol is applied, including role-specific tool listings, nominal and boundary prompts, policy-decision traces, MCP tool-call logs, and inspection of structured outputs injected into the model context. The results show that non-administrative roles received only policy-authorised aggregations and fields in the evaluated scenarios. Relative to the native 5-second resolution, 15-minute aggregation reduced the number of time-series records presented to the model per day from 17,280 to 96, corresponding to a 180-fold record-count reduction. Daily aggregation reduced the number to one record, corresponding to a 17,280-fold record-count reduction. Requests for unauthorised streams or finer temporal resolution were denied. The findings are limited to prototype-level validation of selected governance properties in one office building, using one local LLM configuration and a controlled prompt suite; they do not establish general applicability across buildings, models, or workloads. The evaluation does not assess the numerical accuracy, diagnostic correctness, factual correctness, or reliability of the LLM-generated building-energy analyses. The architecture provides a prototype-level reference design for integrating LLMs into building energy analytics while reducing the number of time-series records presented to the model and improving traceability.
