AB  - With the increasing use of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) in complex scenarios such as material delivery and search and rescue (SAR), the demands for higher levels of intelligence and autonomy continue to grow. However, on resource-constrained UAV platforms, effectively integrating large language models (LLMs) with advanced visual perception to enable fluent natural-language interaction and real-time perception in complex environments remains a significant challenge. To address this problem, this paper proposes a lightweight large-language-model-driven UAV multimodal perception and control system (LMUCS). Centered on a compact language model, the system employs prompt engineering and low-rank adaptation (LoRA) for efficient fine-tuning, enabling accurate instruction parsing from natural-language commands. Quantization is further introduced to support LLM deployment on onboard devices. For visual perception, the system integrates a fine-tuned YOLOv11 object detection model and combines it with MiDaS monocular depth estimation to obtain relative depth cues for target tracking and guided relative approach.In preliminary real-world evaluations, LMUCS demonstrated strong performance and stable operation. On the mode-constrained UAV command benchmark constructed in this study, natural-language instruction parsing achieved an accuracy of 85.49% with Qwen2.5 0.5B and up to 94.68% with Llama3.2 3B. In visual perception tasks, object detection attained a mean average precision (mAP@50-95) of 89.0% and an mAP@50 of 97.8% on the nine-class indoor target dataset developed in this work. Building on these perception and parsing capabilities, the UAV successfully completed basic autonomous material target search and relative target localization/approach tasks in the demonstration scenarios.In addition, this paper benchmarks the key components (LLM and YOLO) on the target edge platform (Jetson Xavier NX) and presents a proof-of-concept result for the integrated system on a PC platform (RTX 4070). Accordingly, the conclusion regarding edge-side feasibility is explicitly bounded as follows: the key modules have been verified to run on the target edge device, whereas the current system-level PoC is intended primarily to validate the multi-module workflow and interaction logic, and does not claim end-to-end real-time closed-loop verification on the target onboard platform. Consistent with the time-scale separation design of LMUCS, the LLM is invoked mainly at low-frequency boundary events, such as task initiation or command updates, while the continuous perception–action loop is executed by the visual perception and control threads. Therefore, the second-level LLM latency on Jetson mainly affects responsiveness to new command inputs and task replanning, rather than the per-frame control frequency. Full end-to-end onboard deployment, together with system-level timing and safety-margin evaluation on the Jetson platform, will be pursued in future work.
