AB  - Process simulators are central to chemical engineering education, yet students often spend more effort mastering software interfaces than engaging with the underlying phenomena. We call this the “simulator interface burden.” Large Language Models offer natural-language interaction but risk generating physically incorrect results for quantitative problems. We present an AI-assisted chromatography tutoring system that addresses both challenges. A Large Language Model connects to a validated Fortran simulator via the Model Context Protocol, so that the tutor reasons from deterministic numerical computation specific to each scenario rather than from probabilistic recall of training data, while natural-language dialogue removes the need for specialized input syntax. The pedagogical architecture implements Predict–Observe–Explain cycles grounded in constructivist learning theory. Students must articulate predictions before simulations execute, which structures the interaction to discourage passive answer consumption and to surface misconceptions for targeted remediation. Progressive scaffolding guides learners from fundamental principles through process optimization. The implementation comprises an MCP server exposing 24 tools for simulation configuration, execution, and analysis, with integrated image serving for chromatogram visualization. The server uses Streamable HTTP transport and is consumed by a purpose-built web application that delivers the curriculum through a browser. Plain-text documents encode the tutor’s pedagogical behavior and a six-laboratory curriculum as modular, reusable specifications. The model system (separation of phenylalanine and tryptophan) provides relevant context while demonstrating nonlinear isotherm effects. An automated evaluation across multiple LLMs (140 sessions) shows the instruction set is robust enough for architecturally diverse models to deliver structured sessions, while faithful reporting of the simulator’s numerical outputs remains strongly model-dependent. The approach offers a replicable pattern for other engineering domains where numerical simulation is central to instruction.
