AB  - The rapid expansion of the Internet of Things (IoT) has created major cybersecurity challenges because of device heterogeneity, limited computational resources, and increasingly complex attack surfaces. Traditional rule-based and signature-based defenses struggle to detect evolving and zero-day threats in such environments. This paper reviews Generative AI (GenAI) for IoT security and introduces a multi-dimensional taxonomy covering Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), diffusion models, transformer models, and Large Language Models (LLMs). A comparative synthesis across major IoT datasets indicates that representative GenAI-based methods consistently report high detection accuracy—often in the 95–100% range— with results varying substantially by dataset characteristics, class balance, and evaluation protocol. The paper also examines technical challenges, adversarial vulnerabilities, privacy concerns, and future directions for adaptive and trustworthy IoT security systems.
