AB  - Knowledge Graphs (KGs) have gained significant attention due to their wide range of applications in knowledge intensive domains. Yet they inherently face the issue of incompleteness and Knowledge Graph Completion (KGC) aims to solve this by predicting missing triples in KG However, applying powerful Large Language Models (LLM) to KGC remains largely unexplored. Existing studies for LLM-based KGC fail to fully exploit essential KG structure information and are ineffective in prompting LLMs to understand highly entangled KG structure, which prevents LLMs from achieving structure-aware reasoning. To tackle above issues, we design a novel framework Knowledge Graph as Prompt (KGaP), which is able to enhance the reasoning and predicting capabilities of LLMs for KG link prediction. Specifically, KGaP first learns disentangled representations from an entity’s multiple semantic aspects through the Disentangled KG Learning, enabling the downstream LLM to adaptively select relevant structure knowledge helpful for the prediction. Subsequently, these derived disentangled cluster representations are optimized and projected into the frozen LLM, with only the prompt parameters being trained, thus reducing computational costs and allowing much more flexibility in combining with different LLM models. This process disentangles complex graph structures and prompts LLM to understand them. We conduct extensive experiments on mainstream benchmarks and analyze KGaP’s efficacy with other baselines. The results show that KGaP achieves state-of-the-art performance, yielding 69.2/47.3 MRR on WN18RR/FB15k-237.
