AB  - Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) has introduced new textual and multimedia data generation capabilities. Recently, several new Text-to-Image/Video (T2I/V) models have been released, demonstrating advanced capabilities in generating realistic images and video from textual prompts. These models have been conceived to maximize their usability across a broad audience, ensuring that users with varying levels of domain expertise can easily interact with them. However, many of these models often fail to meet comprehensive safety criteria, leaving significant gaps in their ability to prevent the generation of unethical or harmful content. This lack of robust safeguards increases the risk that such tools may be misused, intentionally or unintentionally, to produce media that violates ethical standards or propagates misinformation. This research explores the ethical implications of emerging T2I/V models by investigating their ability to generate unethical multimedia content, such as that containing stereotypes, prejudice, violence, and discrimination. To this end, we employ ambiguous prompts, which are seemingly benign or neutral prompts but can be crafted by domain-expert users to bypass the models’ safety mechanisms and indirectly provoke the generation of harmful content. Through user analysis and the evaluation of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) like Claude and Gemini, we find that, while many T2I/V models are designed to avoid generating unethical content, they still produce a significant amount of discriminatory and stereotypical media. A complementary user study involving 50 participants further shows that such content is widely perceived as offensive, non-inclusive, and unsuitable for younger or vulnerable audiences. Conversely, the results demonstrate that Claude Sonnet and Gemini 1.5 Pro show a moderate but promising capacity to analyze and identify unethical elements within multimedia content.
