AB  - Mixture-of-experts (MoE) architectures have become the dominant paradigm for scaling open-source large language models, yet their security implications remain insufficiently understood. Recent studies reveal that MoE safety alignment is concentrated in specific “safety experts” and “safety neurons”, but existing methods require modifying model internals, limiting practical applicability. We identify that a small number of critical tokens in harmful prompts disproportionately activate safety experts at specific layers. Motivated by this insight, we propose ClearCut, a prefix-based jailbreak method that exploits this architectural vulnerability through input manipulation alone while remaining mechanistically interpretable. Specifically, ClearCut optimizes a short adversarial prefix with a hybrid contrastive-suppression loss that redirects these tokens away from safety experts, bypassing safety alignment without any modification to model weights. Across six mainstream MoE models, ClearCut achieves an average success rate of 59.1%, the highest among input-only methods on every model evaluated, outperforming GCG (40.3%) and AutoDAN (45.0%), and approaching the upper-bound reference set by weight-modification methods such as GateBreaker (57.4%). The prefix averages only 5.77 tokens, substantially shorter than existing approaches. By identifying critical tokens and tracking routing changes, ClearCut provides interpretable pathways that reveal how MoE safety mechanisms function. This work exposes a fundamental security risk in MoE architectures, that safety experts can be disproportionately activated and manipulated by a small number of critical tokens. Beyond identifying this vulnerability, we provide ClearCut as a practical tool for security evaluation.
