AB  - Firewall configuration auditing remains manual and time-intensive. Published systems do not span multi-vendor configuration retrieval, Large Language Model (LLM)-based auditing, NIST SP 800-53 mapping and Security Operations Centre (SOC) dispatch in a single pipeline. We present the Network Security Configuration Audit Tool (NSCAT), built on two mechanisms. Hybrid retrieval for control mapping matches each finding against the NIST SP 800-53 catalogue by combining semantic similarity with keyword overlap, so every assignment is grounded in retrieved control text rather than a model’s parametric memory, and is inspectable and measurable. Provenance-aware verification tags a finding Verified where the configuration line it cites is found in the source and AI-inferred where it is not, exposing output unsupported by retrievable evidence to the auditor. Grounding makes measurement possible: across 303 audits the pipeline returned a control for 99.96% of findings, but on a 300-finding stratified sample annotated independently by two annotators strict precision was 39.0% and lenient precision 52.0%. To the best of our knowledge, NSCAT is the first system to measure NIST SP 800-53 mapping precision against an independently annotated ground truth on open-source infrastructure. NSCAT normalises six vendor grammars into a common schema, audits with 36 LLMs across six providers, and dispatches severity-tagged alerts to SIEM and SOAR platforms under role separation between auditor and network team. Configuration review that previously waited for the next audit cycle now takes one operator action and reaches the SOC & network teams within seconds.
