AB  - Smart greenhouses continuously accumulate relational data describing environmental conditions and actuator states, yet operational access to these records often requires knowledge of the database schema and SQL. This creates an accessibility barrier for non-specialist users, while automatic SQL generation introduces additional risks related to semantic mismatches, non-existent schema references, and unauthorised operations. This paper presents QUILLAMA, a traceable natural-language query layer integrated into QUILLAQUA, an Internet of Things (IoT) framework deployed in an experimental smart greenhouse. QUILLAMA implements a natural-language-to-SQL-to-database-to-natural-language (NL → SQL → DB → NL) pipeline orchestrated through Node-RED, accessing the MariaDB QDB repository and using an on-premises Ollama service with Qwen models for SQL generation and response construction. Application-level guardrails restrict execution to a single SELECT statement, block explicitly disallowed operations, and reject chained statements, while schema information, domain synonyms, and retention of the executed SQL support grounding and traceability. At the manuscript-update cut-off, the operational greenhouse repository contained 370 706 records of environmental measurements, derived variables, and actuator states acquired from the monitored cultivation environment. Evaluation used 54 predefined tests covering operational queries, terminological variants, adversarial inputs, and infrastructure failures. Of 32 operational queries, 30 executed without database errors, yielding a 93.8% execution rate. Retrospective semantic assessment classified 19 queries (59.4%) as correct, four (12.5%) as partially correct, and nine (28.1%) as incorrect. Among 19 adversarial attempts, 15 were blocked and four were transformed into SELECT queries; no non-SELECT statement reached MariaDB. The 34.4-percentage-point difference between technical execution and strict semantic correspondence demonstrates that database executability cannot be used as a proxy for interpretation correctness. The results provide preliminary evidence that traceable, read-only natural-language access to operational smart-greenhouse histories is feasible through edge-based orchestration and on-premises inference, while also identifying semantic interpretation as a critical limitation for reliable deployment in controlled-environment agriculture.
