AB  - UAV fleets are increasingly directed through natural-language missions rather than pre-scripted waypoints, yet existing agentic schedulers expose sensitive telemetry to untrusted clouds and cannot guarantee that LLM-generated plans respect battery, deadline, and airspace limits. This paper presents SecAgentSched, a secure agentic framework that encodes security policy as a first-class schedulability constraint, enforces a token-level exposure budget before every external inference call, and closes the loop with a deterministic verifier, LLM-driven repair, and mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) fallback. A four-agent architecture (Mission Interpreter, Constraint Validator, Schedule Synthesizer, Security Auditor) integrates differential privacy, selective AES-256-GCM encryption, and tamper-evident audit logs within a zero-trust edge enclave. We formulate a security-aware multi-UAV scheduling problem with explicit UAV- and station-indexed MEC offloading variables, prove six formal security properties, and evaluate nine baselines over 200 emulated-LLM episodes. A 60-episode live-LLM pilot (Llama-3.2-3B on a Jetson Orin Nano; GPT-4o-mini via cloud API) confirms end-to-end operation: both models achieve 100% task-extraction accuracy and 100% final feasibility through the verifier and MILP fallback. SecAgentSched achieves 100% schedule feasibility, a 98.5% reduction in restricted-token exposure, 0% adversarial success on 200 taxonomy injections, and only 0.5s additional latency over LLM+Verifier.
