AB  - Wireless sensor networks (WSNs) increasingly support sensing-to-decision loops in which the value of a transmission depends on task utility, freshness, uncertainty, privacy, and safety rather than on bit fidelity alone. This article presents a review-led evidence synthesis and, as a secondary contribution, an author-proposed conceptual framework; it does not report a validated control algorithm. The review analyzes a 178-record evidence corpus, complemented by seven separately logged mathematical-outlook references, across WSN and IoT foundations, semantic communication, TinyML and split inference, federated learning, generative AI, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), digital twins, wireless foundation models, and language-model-assisted operations. The supplementary materials release a machine-readable extraction sheet with one row per record, a field codebook, a reference and screening ledger, qualification and completeness audits, and an integrity manifest. A work qualifies as LLM-augmented semantic edge intelligence only when an identifiable large language model (LLM) consumes task-relevant WSN/IoT state or operator intent and can affect sensing, communication, diagnosis, resource allocation, or control through a typed and auditable path. Under this boundary, only one retained work supplies direct E2 simulation evidence of an LLM-mediated communication or control path. Most LLM/RAG evidence is bridging or adjacent and remains at E0–E2. The synthesis therefore distinguishes the roles and maturity of the component technologies instead of treating them as an interchangeable stack. It identifies bounded examples of LLM/RAG utility in communication-policy generation, cross-task networking adaptation, global evidence synthesis, tool orchestration, and natural-language formulation for an external solver. It does not support a general claim of superiority over rules, exact optimization, structured retrieval, or task-specific machine learning and reinforcement learning. To translate the documented gaps into testable requirements, the article proposes an IoT-native reference architecture, a seven-axis taxonomy, a threat model, a formal synthesis and selection/cost model, typed cross-layer interface contracts, and an eight-phase benchmark protocol. These normative artifacts are proposed by the author. They are not established methods extracted from the corpus, consensus standards, a deployable solver or controller, or experimentally validated results. An explicitly hypothetical wildfire-monitoring case makes the formalism inspectable: it defines semantic distortion, normalizes and elicits the penalty weights, evaluates a finite candidate set under hard constraints, applies the non-dominance and LLM-admission rules, reports switch-point sensitivity, and exercises one LLM-to-optimizer interface contract. Its values are tutorial inputs, not measurements. A provenance audit separates cited evidence, author-defined review and coding constructs, conventional notation, and proposed framework artifacts. Each contribution is linked to measurable outcomes and a minimum support condition, but these quantities define future tests rather than imply new performance gains. An LLM is retained only when it closes a declared open-vocabulary or heterogeneous-knowledge capability gap, satisfies hard energy, deadline, privacy, safety, and verifier constraints, and provides positive incremental value over the strongest feasible simpler baseline. Resource-constrained nodes continue to handle sensing, routing, medium access control, and lightweight inference. Grounded LLM/RAG components operate at gateway, edge, cloud, or digital-twin tiers, and their outputs are limited to evidence-linked, tool-checked, reversible proposals. The review remains the evidentiary core; the conceptual framework converts its gaps into falsifiable design and evaluation requirements for future semantic IoT systems.
