AB  - The Cognitive Internet of Medical Things (CIoMT) is reshaping healthcare by enabling continuous monitoring, personalized disease management, and intelligent clinical decision support. CIoMT systems improve diagnostic accuracy, reduce operational costs, and enhance the quality of experience for patients, and healthcare providers. Heterogeneous devices and vendors, multi-layer architectures make CIoMT systems vulnerable to security and privacy breaches. In parallel, the rapid emergence of Generative AI (GenAI), introduces a new duality for CIoMT security: GenAI can both amplify existing threats (e.g., automated exploit generation, sophisticated phishing...) and empower powerful new defense mechanisms (e.g., LLM-assisted vulnerability assessment, log analysis, GenAI-based Security-Operation-Centers, and adaptive policy generation). In this paper, we examine foundations, vulnerabilities, and GenAI-driven defenses in CIoMT systems. We first formalize the CIoMT architectural and data-flow foundations and derive a multi-layer taxonomy of threats and vulnerabilities spanning devices, communication, data, models, and clinical applications. We then analyze how GenAI can be used both to discover and to mitigate vulnerabilities, highlighting concrete defensive use cases as well as GenAI-specific risks. Building on this analysis, we propose a unified security framework for CIoMT in the GenAI era and we identify open research challenges and future directions fostering trustworthy, resilient, and privacy-preserving intelligent healthcare ecosystems.
