AB  - Our study examines privacy concerns and willingness to disclose information to anthropomorphic Large Language Model (LLM)-powered conversational AI agents. Drawing on communication privacy management theory and privacy calculus of perceived risk and perceived value, using scenario-based surveys and structural equation modeling, our model explores how privacy controls, privacy-by-design features, privacy nudges, and anthropomorphic feature interactions influence risks and benefits, affecting disclosure intentions to generative AI agents. Our findings include the impact of anthropomorphic capabilities and privacy nudges as trust enhancers, and the role of institutional assurance such as privacy-by-design, user self regulations such as privacy control settings, and information context sensitivity on concerns and perceived risks. This study contributes to the understanding of privacy behavior in advanced generative AI systems by integrating boundary management and privacy calculus perspectives to establish the concept of information co-ownership and designing guardrails in information sharing to anthropomorphic agents. The study provides insights for design and architecture of LLM-powered privacy-preserving conversational agents that balance personalization and user privacy. Practical implications include incorporating explicit privacy controls, privacy-by-design principles, and increased “personification” such as message framing to improve user trust and information disclosure involving customer-AI engagement. The findings contribute to research and practice by highlighting the complex interplay between privacy, trust, and disclosure in human-AI interactions that can define and shape information exchange in digital commerce and direct-to-consumer services.
