AB  - As artificial intelligence (AI) systems continue to transform critical sectors such as healthcare, finance, autonomous transportation, and national security, their increasing autonomy and complexity have introduced novel cybersecurity vulnerabilities. This paper offers a comprehensive analysis of the evolving threat landscape surrounding AI programs, tracing risk vectors across the full AI lifecycle, from data ingestion and model training to inference and deployment. We explore how foundational features of AI systems, such as large-scale data dependency, black-box model architectures, and automated decision pipelines, inadvertently expand the attack surface. The study categorizes threats into two primary domains: data-centric attacks, including data poisoning and adversarial examples, and model-centric threats, such as backdoor insertion, model inversion, and intellectual property theft via model extraction. Through real-world case studies, including Microsoft’s Tay chatbot, adversarial misclassification of traffic signs, and model leakage from large language models, we illustrate the tangible consequences of unmitigated vulnerabilities. In response, we propose a layered defense strategy grounded in the detect–prevent–predict (DPP) model. This framework advocates for real-time anomaly detection, proactive architectural hardening (e.g., adversarial training, differential privacy, certified robustness), and forward-looking risk prediction through behavioral analytics and monitoring for concept drift. We further discuss the implications of traditional cybersecurity risks, such as phishing, misconfiguration, and Application Programming Interface (API) abuse, as they evolve within AI-specific contexts. Finally, the paper emphasizes that achieving trustworthy AI cannot rely on technical defenses alone. Ethical foresight, regulatory compliance, and interdisciplinary collaboration are essential to developing AI systems that are not only performant, but also resilient, interpretable, and secure. As AI becomes increasingly integrated into sociotechnical ecosystems, this research underscores the urgency of embedding cybersecurity at every stage of the AI design and deployment process.
