AB  - Backdoor attacks play a crucial role in assessing model robustness and privacy risks. However, existing multimodal backdoor methods often fail in high-level downstream tasks such as visual emotion analysis (VEA), as they overlook the issue of semantic asynchrony between different modalities, resulting in suboptimal emotional perception. To address this limitation, we propose EmoAttack, a unified emotion-oriented backdoor attack framework that dynamically optimizes tunable prompts and fine-grained triggers through dual latent space modeling. By exploiting the correlation between emotional semantics and the attack mechanism, our approach optimizes these prompts across different training phases to inject fine-grained target-aware knowledge. As a result, EmoAttack effectively decouples prompt injection from task-specific transfer, facilitating precise emotional cue mining while preserving semantic consistency across diverse visual contents. Extensive experiments on seven public VEA datasets (≈166K images) show that EmoAttack achieves both high stealthiness and stability, with an average clean accuracy improvement of 11.6% and attack success rate (ASR) up to 89.3% even under the CleanClip defense, outperforming state-of-the-art methods.
