AB  - Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative model training without sharing raw data, but its distributed nature exposes it to data poisoning attacks from malicious clients. Evaluating such attacks and corresponding defences requires complex setups that are often implemented through custom scripts, making experiments difficult to reproduce and compare. This paper introduces DPArena, an open-source web platform for reproducible data poisoning experiments in FL. Each submission automatically executes clean, poisoned, clean + defence, and poisoned + defence scenarios and reports per-round metrics. Unlike code-level benchmark suites, DPArena packages reproducibility mechanisms—serialized configurations, managed seeds, pinned environments, standardized exports—behind a guided web interface with static validation of user-submitted code. Two case studies illustrate the platform’s analytical capabilities.
