AB  - Large language models can generate control programs for microcontrollers, the most resource-constrained endpoint of LLM-based edge intelligence. Yet the output must compress to tens of bytes, execute in kilobytes of RAM, and avoid unauthorized hardware access or unbounded execution. Existing approaches address only one side: format validators ensure syntactic correctness but provide no runtime safety, whereas sandboxed runtimes such as WebAssembly exceed typical MCU memory budgets. We present LIR + LVM, a two-layer framework that decouples generation from execution. The LLM emits LIR, a structured intermediate representation, which a deterministic compiler lowers to bytecode. The LVM, a lightweight MCU-resident virtual machine, enforces capability gating and step limits prior to execution. We implement on ESP8266, STM32F103, and ESP32. For security, the LVM contains 8,000 adversarial payloads with no observed escape under the declared-capability model, though semantic and physical safety remain beyond this containment scope. For efficiency, the bytecode is 9.8× smaller than optimized JSON and halves interpretation energy. For reliability, the pipeline achieves a 100% pass rate on deterministic tasks and 0.982 reliability on stochastic tasks. The identical bytecode runs unchanged across three boards — spanning two ISAs and a 32× RAM range — with under 50 lines of adapter code per port.
