AB  - Multi-agent large language models (LLMs) expand capability by delegating subtasks and invoking tools, but they also enlarge the attack surface with instruction-sensitive threats (e.g., prompt hijacking, retrieval poisoning, tool-use redirection) that unfold across agent-to-agent interactions and may look benign in text alone. We present ALTEDA, a log-based Threat Detection and Attribution framework for multi-agent LLMs that does not use user prompts or responses, reducing privacy risk while capturing cross-agent attack signals. ALTEDA red-teams a target workflow to collect synchronized application, network, and system logs, converts each run into an attributed directed multi-graph, and trains a graph-level detector which also supports early compromise detection from partial traces. In addition, our framework attributes detections back to responsible agents and interactions via SHAP with aggregator-aware redistribution. On a red-teamed multi-agent LLM system, we generate 800 traces with a 31.4% attack success rate and show that ALTEDA achieves strong end-to-end detection performance. Our result also shows ALTEDA can surface high-trust early alerts by around 40% trace progress and largely converges toward full performance by mid-trace, enabling intervention before attacks complete. Despite avoiding user content entirely, ALTEDA remains competitive with text-based detectors and incurs low computational overhead, making it suitable for real-time monitoring.
