AB  - The convergence of large language model (LLM)-based autonomous agents, federated learning (FL), and edge computing is creating a distinctive configuration of distributed intelligence that we term Agentic Edge Intelligence (AEI). In AEI, edge nodes operate not as passive gradient producers but as autonomous agents that reason, plan, and learn collaboratively under strict communication and resource constraints. Despite rapid progress in each area, existing surveys address these components in isolation, lacking a unified communication-centric perspective. This article provides a communication-centered synthesis of agentic FL and LLM-driven edge AI (2017–2026), unifying prior work on communication-efficient FL, edge LLM inference, and multi-agent coordination through the lens of the agent communication policy πcomm. We introduce a five-layer AEI reference architecture positioning the communication layer as the primary system constraint. We develop a topology-oriented taxonomy of federated agent architectures—centralized, hierarchical, decentralized peer-to-peer, and hybrid—and quantify their communication complexity, scalability, and fault tolerance. We synthesize communication-efficient collaboration techniques and characterize convergence–communication trade-offs under heterogeneous data and bandwidth constraints. A key formal contribution is the MDP formulation of πcomm, with a proved efficiency bound showing that the utility-threshold policy achieves near-oracle communication cost. We also review communication-aware model optimization—parameter-efficient fine-tuning, distillation, and split inference—and examine security threats specific to agentic federated systems. Open challenges and a research roadmap for communication-efficient distributed intelligence at the network edge close the survey.
