AB  - Large language model (LLM) services are increasingly deployed behind intelligent gateways that enforce access policies and route user requests to heterogeneous backend models. In enterprise and multi-tenant settings, authorization decisions often depend on sensitive user and contextual attributes, such as roles, credentials, and device posture. Revealing these attributes to the gateway may enable user profiling and policy inference. This paper presents PPLLA, a privacy-preserving attribute-based authorization framework tailored to LLM gateways with asymmetric outputs. The client learns only the authorization label(s) needed to unlock corresponding model capabilities, while the gateway learns only the match cardinality to support routing optimization, and the auxiliary server learns no additional information about the client query beyond the prescribed public leakage in the replicated-database model. PPLLA combines a DH-based oblivious PRF to derive pseudorandom item representations, cuckoo hashing to organize a large labeled policy repository, two-server distributed point functions (DPF) to enable private retrieval of a constant number of candidate bins per queried attribute, and a lightweight two-party computation (2PC) to compute the intersection count from XOR-shared tags without revealing which attributes matched.
