AB  - Machine unlearning, the process of selectively removing the influence of specific training data from a trained model without full retraining, has become both a regulatory necessity and a major research frontier. Existing work has largely treated unlearning as a problem of efficiency and utility: how to forget quickly while preserving model performance. This view is incomplete. The same capability that allows a system to honor a deletion request can also be exploited by adversaries to corrupt model behavior through malicious requests, reconstruct data that was meant to be erased, forge evidence of deletion, or weaken model ownership and security guarantees. Machine unlearning should therefore be understood not only as a privacy-preserving mechanism but also as a security-critical process. This survey reframes machine unlearning around this security perspective. It develops a unified threat taxonomy spanning six attack surfaces: membership inference, model inversion, poisoning through unlearning requests, verification forgery, backdoor exploitation, and intellectual property theft. These threats are organized through a five-phase attack life cycle covering target selection, request construction, execution, evasion, and post hoc verification bypass. The survey then maps existing defenses against these threats, identifying where current methods provide protection and where critical gaps remain. The analysis also examines unlearning as a proactive defensive mechanism for backdoor removal, adversarial hardening, copyright protection, and bias correction, showing that each defensive use inherits risks similar to its offensive counterpart. The survey further extends this analysis to large language models, federated learning, graph learning, reinforcement learning, and diffusion models, which do not eliminate the threat surface but fundamentally reshape it, introducing attack vectors that existing unlearning protocols were not designed to address. A consistent pattern emerges: unlearning is most reliable when the target knowledge is localized, identifiable, and verifiably removed, but becomes increasingly fragile when the target is diffuse, distributed, or entangled with knowledge the model must retain. This observation motivates the central contribution of the survey: a privacy–security–utility trilemma, in which no known method simultaneously guarantees privacy, security, and utility under current benchmarks and threat models. The survey concludes by formalizing this trilemma against the latest empirical evidence and outlining a research agenda for achieving verifiable and trustworthy machine unlearning that is secure by design.
