AB  - Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting improves the reasoning capability of large language models on tasks requiring multi-step deduction, but exposing explicit reasoning traces also introduces a surface for reasoning-integrity vulnerabilities. This work studies a controlled data-poisoning setting for systems that expose visible reasoning traces, where models appear to follow normal deduction patterns while ultimately producing incorrect answers. We propose the Chain-of-Thought Truncation Backdoor Attack (CoT-TBA), a training-time attack that induces premature commitment to a targeted error before logical closure when a trigger is present. Unlike approaches that inject malicious reasoning content or bypass reasoning entirely, CoT-TBA preserves a locally coherent reasoning prefix before truncating the deductive process. Experiments across multiple reasoning benchmarks show that CoT-TBA is most effective on tasks with longer explicit reasoning traces, while its effectiveness and utility preservation vary across model architectures and reasoning depth. Additional analyses reveal important boundary conditions: the attack shifts toward sequence elongation on shallow reasoning tasks, and process-level verification using Process Reward Models detects the resulting deductive inconsistency at a high rate. These findings characterize a controlled vulnerability surface for the integrity of explicit reasoning traces under data poisoning, suggesting that lightweight or incomplete inspection of reasoning trajectories may be insufficient without process-level verification.
